WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:05.571
Imagine Torrance considering improvements around an aging commercial district.

2
00:00:05.571 --> 00:00:10.491
Better sidewalks, safer crossings, utilities, and public space could support new investment.

3
00:00:10.491 --> 00:00:14.341
Property owners may gain from improved access and development potential.

4
00:00:14.341 --> 00:00:16.373
Residents may gain jobs and amenities.

5
00:00:16.373 --> 00:00:18.298
The city may face substantial costs.

6
00:00:18.298 --> 00:00:21.186
The policy question is not how to seize private value.

7
00:00:21.186 --> 00:00:25.357
It is how to share publicly created value lawfully, fairly, and transparently.

8
00:00:25.357 --> 00:00:29.528
That is the practical setting for economic development and land value capture.

9
00:00:29.528 --> 00:00:38.939
This episode is educational preparation, not financial, legal, tax, appraisal, economic development, real estate, investment, or municipal advisory advice.

10
00:00:38.939 --> 00:00:46.042
California authority for fees, assessments, districts, property transactions, and development agreements is specific.

11
00:00:46.042 --> 00:00:54.056
Actual decisions require current law, evidence, public process, finance analysis, appraisal when appropriate, and qualified counsel.

12
00:00:54.056 --> 00:00:57.335
Economic development is broader than recruiting firms.

13
00:00:57.335 --> 00:01:08.628
It can improve the conditions under which people and businesses create value: infrastructure, workforce access, safety, permitting, public space, land readiness, and institutional trust.

14
00:01:08.628 --> 00:01:12.938
A good strategy starts with a diagnosed barrier, not a favored subsidy.

15
00:01:12.938 --> 00:01:14.092
Ask five questions.

16
00:01:14.092 --> 00:01:16.703
What market or coordination problem exists?

17
00:01:16.703 --> 00:01:18.645
What public action addresses it?

18
00:01:18.645 --> 00:01:21.013
What would happen without intervention?

19
00:01:21.013 --> 00:01:23.320
Who receives benefits and bears costs?

20
00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:28.177
How will success be measured after accounting for displacement and substitution?

21
00:01:28.177 --> 00:01:33.642
Substitution occurs when activity moves within Torrance rather than adding citywide value.

22
00:01:33.642 --> 00:01:36.859
A new store may attract sales from an existing store.

23
00:01:36.859 --> 00:01:42.870
Displacement occurs when rising costs or redevelopment push out residents, businesses, or services.

24
00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:46.756
Leakage occurs when spending or income leaves the local economy.

25
00:01:46.756 --> 00:01:51.613
These effects do not automatically defeat a project, but they should be visible.

26
00:01:51.613 --> 00:02:02.602
Set objectives that describe outcomes: productive reuse of underused land, safer access, more housing choices, retained jobs, stronger small businesses, or resilient infrastructure.

27
00:02:02.602 --> 00:02:08.977
Avoid using ribbon cuttings, construction spending, or gross square footage as the only success measures.

28
00:02:08.977 --> 00:02:19.630
Land value capture refers to public mechanisms that recover a portion of land value increases associated with public action, infrastructure, or granted development opportunity.

29
00:02:19.630 --> 00:02:25.017
The recovered value can help fund improvements or public benefits related to that growth.

30
00:02:25.017 --> 00:02:27.075
The phrase covers different tools.

31
00:02:27.075 --> 00:02:40.693
Depending on law and facts, examples may include benefit assessments, development impact fees, negotiated development agreements, infrastructure financing districts, public land leases, joint development, or other mechanisms.

32
00:02:40.693 --> 00:02:42.872
These tools are not interchangeable.

33
00:02:42.872 --> 00:02:48.017
They differ in legal basis, nexus, payer, geography, timing, risk, and permitted use.

34
00:02:48.017 --> 00:02:50.378
Separate three sources of value change.

35
00:02:50.378 --> 00:02:53.767
First is general market change, such as regional demand.

36
00:02:53.767 --> 00:02:58.186
Second is owner-created value, such as private investment and management.

37
00:02:58.186 --> 00:03:04.602
Third is public-action value, such as infrastructure, rezoning, assembly, remediation, or improved access.

38
00:03:04.602 --> 00:03:06.115
In reality they interact.

39
00:03:06.115 --> 00:03:10.897
A defensible approach does not casually attribute every increase to government.

40
00:03:10.897 --> 00:03:13.136
Next, define the public contribution.

41
00:03:13.136 --> 00:03:19.673
List land, infrastructure, regulatory change, financing, risk assumption, staff effort, and ongoing service.

42
00:03:19.673 --> 00:03:21.731
Value each contribution carefully.

43
00:03:21.731 --> 00:03:31.718
Then identify the public return: cash, infrastructure, affordable space, public access, environmental remediation, revenue participation, or performance commitments.

44
00:03:31.718 --> 00:03:32.626
Timing matters.

45
00:03:32.626 --> 00:03:36.863
Value may appear only after entitlements, construction, and occupancy.

46
00:03:36.863 --> 00:03:40.010
Collecting too early can prevent a feasible project.

47
00:03:40.010 --> 00:03:43.945
Waiting too long can leave the public with no practical leverage.

48
00:03:43.945 --> 00:03:51.208
Phasing, reimbursement agreements, milestones, and contingent payments can align timing, but each introduces complexity.

49
00:03:51.208 --> 00:03:53.266
Fairness matters as much as yield.

50
00:03:53.266 --> 00:04:06.098
Ask whether similarly situated parties are treated consistently, whether small owners can participate, whether renters or local businesses face indirect burdens, and whether benefits reach affected neighborhoods.

51
00:04:06.098 --> 00:04:08.216
Publish the method and assumptions.

52
00:04:08.216 --> 00:04:12.801
Consider a hypothetical Torrance district around several underused parcels.

53
00:04:12.801 --> 00:04:16.530
The city is evaluating streetscape work and utility upgrades.

54
00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:19.342
Private owners could redevelop over ten years.

55
00:04:19.342 --> 00:04:21.176
Begin with the no-action case.

56
00:04:21.176 --> 00:04:24.355
What investment is likely without city improvements?

57
00:04:24.355 --> 00:04:28.573
Then define the public project and estimate its full life-cycle cost.

58
00:04:28.573 --> 00:04:32.302
Map beneficiaries by parcel, user group, and citywide effect.

59
00:04:32.302 --> 00:04:35.358
Identify legal tools only after that factual work.

60
00:04:35.358 --> 00:04:37.315
For each tool, create a profile.

61
00:04:37.315 --> 00:04:43.672
A fee may depend on a demonstrated relationship to impacts and cannot simply collect any desired amount.

62
00:04:43.672 --> 00:04:48.929
An assessment generally requires analysis of special benefit and a prescribed process.

63
00:04:48.929 --> 00:04:56.510
A negotiated agreement may address project-specific obligations but depends on bargaining, authority, and enforceable terms.

64
00:04:56.510 --> 00:05:03.112
A district mechanism may direct defined future revenues but exposes the city to timing and performance risk.

65
00:05:03.112 --> 00:05:10.264
A public lease may preserve ownership while producing income, but it requires asset management and market discipline.

66
00:05:10.264 --> 00:05:21.635
Compare the tools using ten criteria: authority, revenue potential, predictability, timing, administration, transaction cost, equity, market effect, accountability, and failure response.

67
00:05:21.635 --> 00:05:23.774
Do not count projected value twice.

68
00:05:23.774 --> 00:05:28.848
If future revenue supports debt, it cannot also be treated as free operating money.

69
00:05:28.848 --> 00:05:35.145
If a developer builds an improvement, clarify whether that satisfies a fee obligation or is additional.

70
00:05:35.145 --> 00:05:38.996
If a parcel already produces revenue, count only the increment.

71
00:05:38.996 --> 00:05:40.035
Build guardrails.

72
00:05:40.035 --> 00:05:42.542
Use independent valuation where material.

73
00:05:42.542 --> 00:05:44.681
Disclose assumptions and conflicts.

74
00:05:44.681 --> 00:05:47.554
Require competitive processes when appropriate.

75
00:05:47.554 --> 00:05:49.939
Tie incentives to verified performance.

76
00:05:49.939 --> 00:05:53.362
Include clawbacks or remedies when lawful and practical.

77
00:05:53.362 --> 00:05:55.379
Define ownership and maintenance.

78
00:05:55.379 --> 00:05:56.663
Set expiration dates.

79
00:05:56.663 --> 00:05:58.069
Report actual outcomes.

80
00:05:58.069 --> 00:06:01.449
Economic development incentives deserve a but-for test.

81
00:06:01.449 --> 00:06:07.287
Would the desired outcome occur at the same scale, quality, place, and time without assistance?

82
00:06:07.287 --> 00:06:14.538
The answer is rarely certain, so document evidence and use the least public contribution needed to achieve the result.

83
00:06:14.538 --> 00:06:17.611
Avoid paying for activity that was already likely.

84
00:06:17.611 --> 00:06:19.762
Also apply a public portfolio view.

85
00:06:19.762 --> 00:06:25.538
One attractive project can consume staff, land, funds, or borrowing capacity needed elsewhere.

86
00:06:25.538 --> 00:06:34.388
Compare proposed support with small-business assistance, infrastructure maintenance, process improvements, housing programs, or no intervention.

87
00:06:34.388 --> 00:06:36.047
Finally, set a review date.

88
00:06:36.047 --> 00:06:38.935
Market conditions and public priorities change.

89
00:06:38.935 --> 00:06:45.265
A mechanism designed for one forecast should not continue automatically after its rationale disappears.

90
00:06:45.265 --> 00:06:51.656
Your learner artifact is a Public Value Sharing Term Sheet for a hypothetical Torrance district project.

91
00:06:51.656 --> 00:06:54.851
Include: The diagnosed problem and public objective.

92
00:06:54.851 --> 00:06:56.265
The no-action baseline.

93
00:06:56.265 --> 00:06:58.846
Public contributions and estimated timing.

94
00:06:58.846 --> 00:07:01.119
Private contributions and milestones.

95
00:07:01.119 --> 00:07:03.639
Likely beneficiaries and burdened groups.

96
00:07:03.639 --> 00:07:05.298
Value creation assumptions.

97
00:07:05.298 --> 00:07:09.538
Candidate capture mechanisms, with authority marked for legal review.

98
00:07:09.538 --> 00:07:11.566
Public return and permitted uses.

99
00:07:11.566 --> 00:07:12.857
Performance measures.

100
00:07:12.857 --> 00:07:14.516
Maintenance responsibility.

101
00:07:14.516 --> 00:07:17.404
Risk allocation, remedies, and exit conditions.

102
00:07:17.404 --> 00:07:18.572
Reporting schedule.

103
00:07:18.572 --> 00:07:27.421
End with a paragraph explaining why your preferred mechanism is proportional to the public contribution and more suitable than two alternatives.

104
00:07:27.421 --> 00:07:29.142
Now, three recall questions.

105
00:07:29.142 --> 00:07:31.538
Question one: What is the but-for test?

106
00:07:31.538 --> 00:07:39.466
Answer: It asks whether the desired outcome would occur at the same scale, quality, location, and time without public assistance.

107
00:07:39.466 --> 00:07:44.628
Question two: Why should all land value increase not be attributed to public action?

108
00:07:44.628 --> 00:07:50.589
Answer: General market forces and private investment also create value, and the sources interact.

109
00:07:50.589 --> 00:07:53.846
Question three: What makes value capture accountable?

110
00:07:53.846 --> 00:08:02.695
Answer: Clear authority, transparent valuation, proportionality, defined permitted uses, enforceable terms, performance reporting, and remedies.

111
00:08:02.695 --> 00:08:18.418
This episode used the public nine-module outline of the Lincoln Institute of Land Policy's Foundations of Local Government Finance in the United States as topic guidance, particularly its listed economic development and land value capture modules.

112
00:08:18.418 --> 00:08:22.811
No account-gated Lincoln lesson was accessed or claimed as extracted.

113
00:08:22.811 --> 00:08:28.540
CDIAC's public Elect>Ed outline informed only the caution about commitments and financing.

114
00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:30.704
No gated Elect>Ed lesson was used.

115
00:08:30.704 --> 00:08:48.082
Concepts concerning municipal assets, land-based finance, public-private arrangements, and external resources were adapted from Municipal Finances: A Handbook for Local Governments, edited by Catherine Farvacque-Vitkovic and Mihaly Kopanyi, World Bank, 2014, CC BY 3.0 IGO.

116
00:08:48.082 --> 00:08:57.440
Changes include original California legal cautions, a Torrance district scenario, a but-for test, displacement analysis, and a term-sheet exercise.

117
00:08:57.440 --> 00:09:00.495
The World Bank does not endorse this adaptation.

118
00:09:00.495 --> 00:09:11.954
Additional sources include Institute for Local Government finance resources and City of Torrance public budgets, Capital Improvement Plans, ACFRs, dashboards, and agenda materials.

119
00:09:11.954 --> 00:09:14.755
Hypothetical details are not Torrance facts.

120
00:09:14.755 --> 00:09:21.439
Economic development succeeds when public action solves a real problem and produces durable public value.

121
00:09:21.439 --> 00:09:29.141
Land value capture is one possible funding approach, not a shortcut around authority, evidence, fairness, or stewardship.

122
00:09:29.141 --> 00:09:32.607
Next, we examine operating costs after the ribbon cutting.
